NAME
====

**ecstatic** - generate a static site

SYNOPSIS
========

**ecstatic** **build** [**-s** _dir_ | **--source** _dir_]
         [**-d** _dir_ | **--destination** _dir_] [**-q** | **--quiet**]

**ecstatic** **serve** [**-d** _dir_ | **--directory** _dir_] [**-H** _host_ | **--host** _host_]
         [**-p** _port_ | **--port** _port_] [**-q** | **--quiet**]

**ecstatic** [**-v** | **--version**]

**ecstatic** [**-h** | **--help**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**ecstatic** is a static site generator. A site is a directory of source files
and a theme, a Wattle file that turns the site into pages.

Subcommands
-----------

- **build**:
  Build the site in the source directory and write its pages to the
  destination directory.

- **serve**:
  Serve a built site over HTTP, for previewing it. It answers GET and HEAD
  requests. A path ending in ``/`` gives the directory's ``index.html``, a
  directory named without the ``/`` is redirected, and a path with no file
  gives the file with ``.html`` added if there is one. Each request is
  printed with its status. It runs until it is stopped.

Build options
-------------

- **-s** _dir_ | **--source** _dir_:
  The directory of the site's source files. This defaults to the current
  working directory.

- **-d** _dir_ | **--destination** _dir_:
  The directory to write the site into. This defaults to ``../public``,
  relative to the current working directory. Missing directories are created.

- **-q** | **--quiet**:
  Suppress progress output.

Serve options
-------------

- **-d** _dir_ | **--directory** _dir_:
  The directory to serve. This defaults to ``../public``, relative to the
  current working directory, the default destination of **build**.

- **-H** _host_ | **--host** _host_:
  The address to listen on. This defaults to ``127.0.0.1``.

- **-p** _port_ | **--port** _port_:
  The port to listen on. This defaults to ``4000``. With ``0``, a free port is
  chosen and printed.

- **-q** | **--quiet**:
  Suppress the log of requests.

General options
---------------

- **-v** | **--version**:
  Print the version and exit.

- **-h** | **--help**:
  Print a help message and exit.

THEMES
======

The theme is the file ``_ecstatic.wattle`` in the source directory. It is
compiled when the site is built, so it can be changed without rebuilding
**ecstatic**, and it can import files beside it by relative path.

The theme must define a function, ``build``, that takes a map of the site and
returns a vector of pages. Each page is a map with ``:path``, the path of the
file relative to the destination directory, and ``:body``, its contents as a
string or buffer. A path cannot be absolute or contain ``..``. If any page is
invalid, no page is written.

The pages and the site's static files are written to a new directory beside
the destination, ``.<name>.ecstatic-new``, which then replaces the
destination. A failed build leaves the previous site in place. A page cannot
have the path of a static file or of another page. The destination cannot be
the source directory or contain it.

The theme can define ``config``, a map that is passed to ``build`` as
``:config``. **ecstatic** reads these keys from it:

- ``:offset``:
  The offset from UTC of the site's dates, such as ``"+0900"``. It applies to
  dates in URLs and to dates written without an offset. This defaults to
  ``"+0000"``.

- ``:include``:
  A vector of paths beginning with ``_`` to read, such as ``["_pages"]``.

- ``:exclude``:
  A vector of paths to skip, such as ``["Gemfile"]``.

- ``:future?``:
  Whether to include posts dated in the future. This defaults to false.

- ``:markdown``:
  A map with ``:options``, a vector of cmark options, and ``:transforms``, a
  vector of functions. See `<MARKDOWN>` below.

- ``:keep``:
  A vector of paths in the destination to carry over to the new site, such
  as files that Git tracks. This defaults to ``[".git" ".gitkeep" ".svn"]``.

The theme can also define ``permalink``, a function that takes a post and
returns its URL. Without it, a post's URL is
``/<categories>/<yyyy>/<mm>/<dd>/<slug>.html``. A ``permalink`` in a post's
front matter takes precedence over both.

CONTENT
=======

The source directory is read as Jekyll reads it:

- A file under ``_posts`` named ``YYYY-MM-DD-slug.ext`` that begins with
  front matter is a post. Subdirectories of ``_posts`` are read.
- Any other file that begins with front matter is a page.
- Any other file is static.

A file or directory whose name begins with ``_`` or ``.`` is skipped unless it
is under a path in ``:include``. A post or page whose front matter sets
``published`` to false is skipped.

Front matter is YAML between lines of ``---``. Only a subset of YAML is read:
plain, single-quoted and double-quoted scalars, flow lists such as
``[a, b]``, nested maps and literal block scalars. Other syntax is an error.

The site map passed to ``build`` has these keys:

- ``:config``, ``:source`` and ``:destination``.
- ``:revision``, the commit hash of ``HEAD`` in the repository containing the
  source directory, or nil, and ``:time``, when the build started.
- ``:posts``, newest first. Each post has ``:path`` (relative to the source
  directory), ``:url``, ``:slug``, ``:date`` (seconds since the epoch),
  ``:title``, ``:categories``, ``:tags``, ``:front-matter``, ``:content``
  (the text after the front matter), ``:html``, ``:excerpt`` and
  ``:modified``, the time of the last commit to change the file or, if there
  is none, the time the file was modified.
- ``:pages``. Each page has ``:path``, ``:url``, ``:title``,
  ``:front-matter``, ``:content``, ``:html``, ``:excerpt`` and ``:modified``. A page's URL is
  its ``permalink`` or its path, with Markdown files ending in ``.html`` and
  ``index.html`` giving the URL of its directory.
- ``:static``, the paths of the static files.

MARKDOWN
========

Posts and pages whose paths end in ``.md``, ``.markdown``, ``.mkdown``,
``.mkdn`` or ``.mkd`` are converted from Markdown with cmark-gfm into
``:html``. Other posts and pages have their content as ``:html``. The
``:excerpt`` is the ``excerpt`` in the front matter or, for Markdown, the HTML
of the text before the first blank line.

The ``:options`` of the Markdown configuration are keywords for cmark's
options: ``:footnotes``, ``:smart``, ``:unsafe``, ``:hardbreaks``,
``:nobreaks``, ``:sourcepos``, ``:validate-utf8``, ``:github-pre-lang``,
``:liberal-html-tag`` and ``:full-info-string``. Without ``:unsafe``, raw HTML
is left out.

Each of the ``:transforms`` is called with the document node after parsing
and before rendering. It edits the tree with these functions, which the theme
can use without importing anything:

- ``cmark/nodes``, ``cmark/children`` and ``cmark/parent``, which find nodes.
  ``cmark/nodes`` returns the node and its descendants in document order, as
  they were when it was called.
- ``cmark/type``, which returns a node's type as a keyword, such as
  ``:paragraph``, ``:text``, ``:code-block`` or ``:footnote-reference``.
- ``cmark/literal``, ``cmark/fence-info``, ``cmark/url``, ``cmark/title`` and
  ``cmark/heading-level``, and the setters ``cmark/set-literal!``,
  ``cmark/set-fence-info!``, ``cmark/set-url!`` and ``cmark/set-title!``.
- ``cmark/new``, which makes a node of a type in the same document, and
  ``cmark/insert-before!``, ``cmark/insert-after!``, ``cmark/append-child!``,
  ``cmark/prepend-child!`` and ``cmark/unlink!``, which move nodes.

A theme can also call ``markdown/render`` and ``markdown/inline``, which
renders Markdown without ``<p>`` tags, with a Markdown configuration.

HELPERS
=======

A theme can use these functions without importing anything. Each has a
docstring.

- ``hiccup/html``, ``hiccup/html5`` and ``hiccup/xml`` write Hiccup: an
  element is a vector of a tag keyword (with an ID and classes, as in
  ``:div#main.post``), an optional map of attributes and the children. Text
  is escaped; ``hiccup/raw`` marks HTML that is not.
- ``date/format`` formats a time at an offset with strftime directives, such
  as ``"%-d %B %Y"``. ``date/rfc3339`` and ``date/rfc822`` give the forms
  feeds use.
- ``paginate/pages`` splits items into numbered pages.
- ``feed/atom``, ``feed/rss`` and ``feed/json`` write feeds.
- ``highlight/load-grammars`` loads the TextMate grammars (``.json`` files)
  in a directory into a map of scope names to grammars, and
  ``highlight/lines`` returns the HTML of each line of code highlighted with
  a grammar. Each token is a ``<span>`` with a class such as
  ``hl-keyword``, ``hl-string`` or ``hl-comment``. A Markdown transform can
  use them to replace code blocks.

EXAMPLES
========

A theme that writes one page:

````
(def config {:title "Example"})

(defn build [site]
  [{:path "index.html"
    :body (string "<h1>" (get-in site [:config :title]) "</h1>")}])
````

Build the site in ``src`` into ``public``:

````
$ cd src
$ ecstatic build
````

EXIT STATUS
===========

**ecstatic** exits with 0 on success and 1 if an error occurs.
